### What is the Command pattern?

The Command pattern encapsulates a request as an object, so an action, its parameters, and its receiver are bundled together. This lets you store, pass, queue, log, and undo operations.

```java
package com.bank.command;

public interface Command {
    void execute();
    void undo();
}
```

### How do you implement concrete commands?

A concrete command holds a reference to the receiver and the parameters. Its `execute` calls the receiver's real method, and its `undo` performs the reverse.

```java
package com.bank.command;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class DebitCommand implements Command {
    private final Account account;
    private final BigDecimal amount;

    public DebitCommand(Account account, BigDecimal amount) {
        this.account = account;
        this.amount = amount;
    }

    @Override
    public void execute() {
        account.debit(amount);
        System.out.println("Debited INR " + amount + " from " + account.getId());
    }

    @Override
    public void undo() {
        account.credit(amount);
        System.out.println("Reversed debit of INR " + amount + " to " + account.getId());
    }
}

public class CreditCommand implements Command {
    private final Account account;
    private final BigDecimal amount;

    public CreditCommand(Account account, BigDecimal amount) {
        this.account = account;
        this.amount = amount;
    }

    @Override
    public void execute() {
        account.credit(amount);
        System.out.println("Credited INR " + amount + " to " + account.getId());
    }

    @Override
    public void undo() {
        account.debit(amount);
        System.out.println("Reversed credit of INR " + amount + " from " + account.getId());
    }
}
```

### How does the invoker enable undo?

The invoker keeps a history stack. Each time a command executes, it is pushed onto the stack. Undo pops the last command and calls its `undo()`. A redo stack enables redo.

```java
package com.bank.command;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class TransactionInvoker {
    private final Deque<Command> history = new ArrayDeque<>();
    private final Deque<Command> redoStack = new ArrayDeque<>();

    public void execute(Command cmd) {
        cmd.execute();
        history.push(cmd);
        redoStack.clear(); // new action invalidates redo
    }

    public void undo() {
        if (history.isEmpty()) throw new IllegalStateException("Nothing to undo");
        Command cmd = history.pop();
        cmd.undo();
        redoStack.push(cmd);
    }

    public void redo() {
        if (redoStack.isEmpty()) throw new IllegalStateException("Nothing to redo");
        Command cmd = redoStack.pop();
        cmd.execute();
        history.push(cmd);
    }
}
```

```java
TransactionInvoker invoker = new TransactionInvoker();
Account rajesh = new Account("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("100000"));

invoker.execute(new DebitCommand(rajesh, new BigDecimal("25000")));
invoker.execute(new CreditCommand(rajesh, new BigDecimal("5000")));
invoker.undo(); // reverses the credit
invoker.undo(); // reverses the debit
```

```text
Debited INR 25000 from SAV2001
Credited INR 5000 to SAV2001
Reversed credit of INR 5000 from SAV2001
Reversed debit of INR 25000 to SAV2001
```

### How does queueing work?

Because commands are objects, they can be put on a queue and executed later by a worker. This enables batch processing, scheduling, and retry.

```java
package com.bank.command;

import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Queue;

public class BatchProcessor {
    private final Queue<Command> queue = new LinkedList<>();

    public void enqueue(Command cmd) { queue.add(cmd); }

    public void processAll() {
        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            queue.poll().execute();
        }
    }
}
```

```java
BatchProcessor batch = new BatchProcessor();
batch.enqueue(new DebitCommand(rajesh, new BigDecimal("10000")));
batch.enqueue(new CreditCommand(priya, new BigDecimal("10000")));
batch.processAll(); // processes transfer as a batch
```

### How does logging work?

Each command can describe or serialise itself, so you write it to an audit log before executing. This gives a replayable trail.

```java
public void execute(Command cmd) {
    auditLog.write(cmd.toString()); // log before execution
    cmd.execute();
    history.push(cmd);
}
// The audit log can be replayed to reconstruct state
```

### How do macro commands work?

A macro command composes several commands into one. Its `execute` runs them in order, and its `undo` runs their undos in reverse order.

```java
package com.bank.command;

import java.util.List;

public class TransferMacro implements Command {
    private final List<Command> steps;

    public TransferMacro(Account from, Account to,
                         java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        this.steps = List.of(
            new DebitCommand(from, amount),
            new CreditCommand(to, amount)
        );
    }

    @Override
    public void execute() {
        steps.forEach(Command::execute);
    }

    @Override
    public void undo() {
        // Undo in reverse order
        for (int i = steps.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            steps.get(i).undo();
        }
    }
}
```

```java
Command transfer = new TransferMacro(rajesh, priya, new BigDecimal("50000"));
transfer.execute(); // debits rajesh, credits priya
transfer.undo();    // credits rajesh, debits priya
```

### What are common mistakes?

The first mistake is not capturing enough state for undo. For a transfer, the command must store both accounts and the amount, not just the action.

The second mistake is not guarding against double-undo, which would reverse an already-reversed operation.

```java
// Wrong: undo can be called multiple times
cmd.undo(); // first undo: correct
cmd.undo(); // second undo: double-reversal, wrong balance

// Correct: track execution state
private boolean executed = false;
@Override
public void execute() {
    if (executed) throw new IllegalStateException("Already executed");
    account.debit(amount);
    executed = true;
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

In modern Java, a simple Command is often just a `Runnable`, which is why task queues and executors are Command-style. The pattern earns its full weight when you need undo, auditability, or composition.

For money, undo correctness is critical. Pankaj insists on exact inverse operations, guards against double-undo, and keeps commands immutable and self-contained so they are safe to queue and replay across threads.

```java
// Modern Java: Command as Runnable
Runnable cmd = () -> account.debit(new BigDecimal("10000"));
executorService.submit(cmd); // queued for later execution

// Full Command pattern: when you need undo and audit
Command cmd = new DebitCommand(account, new BigDecimal("10000"));
invoker.execute(cmd); // logged, undoable, composable
```
